@*
	Every glyph the UI uses, defined once per page as <symbol>s that <Icon> points at.

	Inlined, and that is not a preference. The page is served under a strict content policy, and a
	<use href="sprite.svg#id"> is a cross-document reference the browser refuses without a word in the
	console: the icons would simply stop existing on the deployed machine while looking perfect in
	development. Inline symbols are same-document, so there is nothing to fetch and nothing to forbid.

	Inlining also keeps the two-tone technique alive. The glyphs are drawn in the 0z0 house language
	(docs/design/icons/README.md): a currentColor outline plus one flat accent bound to
	var(--icon-accent, currentColor). Both bindings need the glyph to be part of the page's own DOM —
	an <img src> would isolate it from the cascade and render flat black in both themes.

	Sourced from docs/design/icons/*.svg. The <symbol> keeps the source file's presentation attributes
	verbatim; only the <svg> wrapper becomes <symbol id> so a copy back and forth stays a copy.

	Hosted once by MainLayout. A page must not host its own: two copies means two elements with the same
	id, and the browser resolves to whichever came first.
*@

<svg class="icon-sprite" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
	<defs>
		@* --- product and section marks --- *@

		@* The whole product in one glyph: a point of light answering motion waves. *@
		<symbol id="i-app" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round">
			<circle cx="10" cy="12" r="2.2" fill="var(--icon-accent, currentColor)" stroke="none" />
			<line x1="6.2" y1="12" x2="4.4" y2="12" />
			<line x1="7.4" y1="9.4" x2="6.1" y2="8.1" />
			<line x1="7.4" y1="14.6" x2="6.1" y2="15.9" />
			<path d="M14.6 8.4 A5.8 5.8 0 0 1 14.6 15.6" />
			<path d="M17.3 6.2 A9.3 9.3 0 0 1 17.3 17.8" />
		</symbol>

		@* Rooms as places: a floor plan with a door gap and the light on in one room. *@
		<symbol id="i-areas" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linejoin="round">
			<rect x="4" y="4.5" width="16" height="15" rx="1.6" />
			<line x1="13" y1="4.5" x2="13" y2="12" />
			<line x1="4" y1="12" x2="9.5" y2="12" />
			<circle cx="16.5" cy="8.2" r="1.9" fill="var(--icon-accent, currentColor)" stroke="none" />
		</symbol>

		@* The circadian brightness curve with the sun at solar noon — the exact chart the card draws. *@
		<symbol id="i-schedule" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round">
			<path d="M3.5 18.5 C7.5 18.5 7.5 9.5 12 9.5 C16.5 9.5 16.5 18.5 20.5 18.5" />
			<circle cx="12" cy="5.2" r="1.9" fill="var(--icon-accent, currentColor)" stroke="none" />
		</symbol>

		@* A rotary selector: one mode at a time, chosen deliberately. Preferred over the segmented
		   alternative, which would have collided with the app's own segmented controls. *@
		<symbol id="i-modes" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round">
			<circle cx="12" cy="12" r="5.6" />
			<line x1="6.9" y1="6.9" x2="5.8" y2="5.8" stroke="var(--icon-accent, currentColor)" />
			<line x1="17.1" y1="6.9" x2="18.2" y2="5.8" />
			<line x1="6.9" y1="17.1" x2="5.8" y2="18.2" />
			<line x1="17.1" y1="17.1" x2="18.2" y2="18.2" />
			<line x1="12" y1="12" x2="8.7" y2="8.7" stroke="var(--icon-accent, currentColor)" stroke-width="1.8" />
			<circle cx="12" cy="12" r="1.3" fill="var(--icon-accent, currentColor)" stroke="none" />
		</symbol>

		@* The installation itself, with the master switch inside it. *@
		<symbol id="i-house" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
			<path d="M4 10.8 L12 4.3 L20 10.8" />
			<path d="M6.2 9.4 V19.5 H17.8 V9.4" />
			<line x1="12" y1="10.4" x2="12" y2="13.9" stroke="var(--icon-accent, currentColor)" />
			<path d="M14.2 11.9 A3 3 0 1 1 9.8 11.9" stroke="var(--icon-accent, currentColor)" />
		</symbol>

		@* Who lives here. Ships beside i-house rather than instead of it: two house outlines on one page
		   is a deliberate rhyme, each glyph exactly literal about its own card. *@
		<symbol id="i-residents" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
			<path d="M4 10.8 L12 4.3 L20 10.8" />
			<path d="M6.2 9.4 V19.5 H17.8 V9.4" />
			<circle cx="12" cy="11.9" r="1.9" fill="var(--icon-accent, currentColor)" stroke="none" />
			<path d="M8.9 17.6 A3.1 3.1 0 0 1 15.1 17.6" />
		</symbol>

		@* Commissioned by ui-design-c §6.2, which refused both drawn dashboard glyphs — tiles and gauge
		   are the wrong metaphor for a home surface that is a timeline, and a wrong metaphor is worse
		   than none. Three tracks of unequal length, cut by an accent now-line: the board in miniature. *@
		<symbol id="i-lanes" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round">
			<line x1="3.7" y1="7.2" x2="17.6" y2="7.2" />
			<line x1="3.7" y1="12" x2="12.4" y2="12" />
			<line x1="3.7" y1="16.8" x2="20.3" y2="16.8" />
			<line x1="15.2" y1="4.4" x2="15.2" y2="19.6" stroke="var(--icon-accent, currentColor)" stroke-width="1.8" />
		</symbol>

		@* --- state glyphs ---
		   Stroke 2 rather than 1.7: these live on 13-15px chips, and the library's optical-size rule asks
		   for the bolder cut below ~16px. Their accent is overridden to currentColor at the point of use
		   so the shape and the semantic colour say one thing. *@

		@* The automation running by itself: a loop around the light point. *@
		<symbol id="i-auto" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
			<path d="M18.8 12 A6.8 6.8 0 1 1 15.4 6.1" />
			<path d="M12.6 6 L15.4 6.1 L14 3.5" />
			<circle cx="12" cy="12" r="2" fill="var(--icon-accent, currentColor)" stroke="none" />
		</symbol>

		@* A human set this: a person over the light. *@
		<symbol id="i-manual" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round">
			<circle cx="12" cy="7.4" r="3.1" fill="var(--icon-accent, currentColor)" stroke="none" />
			<path d="M5.4 19 A6.6 6.6 0 0 1 18.6 19" />
		</symbol>

		@* Disengaged: a circle struck through. Preferred over a power symbol, which reads "toggle me"
		   and would sit next to real toggles. *@
		<symbol id="i-off" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round">
			<circle cx="12" cy="12" r="7.6" />
			<line x1="16.6" y1="6.6" x2="7.4" y2="17.4" stroke="var(--icon-accent, currentColor)" />
		</symbol>

		@* The fourth state glyph ui-design-c §6.2 held in reserve, drawn: the warning dim.

		   The design shipped it as the auto loop in amber, blinking, and named that a compromise —
		   truthfully, since PreOff is the engine acting. But sharing a shape means that in greyscale, and
		   for a colourblind reader, "lit and settled" and "about to go dark" are the same picture, which
		   is the exact failure the state glyphs exist to fix. So it gets its own shape: the light with a
		   quarter of its time left, the same draining ring the room header already draws as a countdown.
		   Nothing existing was bent into a new meaning; the amber and the blink still apply. *@
		<symbol id="i-dimming" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round">
			<circle cx="12" cy="12" r="7.6" />
			<path d="M12 12 L12 5.6 A6.4 6.4 0 0 1 18.4 12 Z" fill="var(--icon-accent, currentColor)" stroke="none" />
		</symbol>
	</defs>
</svg>
